## 🅰️ Interview Q&A {#interview-qa}
| Question | Ideal Answer |
|---|---|
| **What does this project do?** | “It authenticates a user with Spotify, fetches their personal listening data via the Spotify Web API, and displays it with a polished React UI. The back‑end handles the OAuth token exchange securely.” |
| **Why is the token exchange done on the server?** | “The client secret must never be exposed to the browser; the server can keep it secret and safely request access/refresh tokens from Spotify.” |
| **Explain the `state` parameter.** | “A random string stored in a cookie to mitigate CSRF attacks. When Spotify redirects back, we compare the returned state with the cookie; a mismatch aborts the flow.” |
| **What is `connect-history-api-fallback` for?** | “It rewrites unknown paths to `index.html` so that client‑side routes (e.g., `/top-tracks`) work when the user refreshes or directly navigates to them.” |
| **How does the app scale on the back‑end?** | “We use Node’s `cluster` module: the master forks a worker per CPU core, allowing concurrent request handling across all cores.” |
| **If you wanted to add dark‑mode, where would you start?** | “Swap the `theme` object with a dark‑mode variant and pass it via a ThemeProvider; all styled‑components read from the theme, so the UI updates automatically.” |
| **Potential improvements?** | “Store tokens in HttpOnly secure cookies, write unit/integration tests for the server routes, replace the deprecated `request` library with `node-fetch`, and add pagination for large data sets.” |
